   <dc:title>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Characterization of the Sensitivity and Survey Potential of a Commercial Metal Detector Mounted on a Robotic Platform for Safely Detecting Plastic Landmines</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">Characterization of the Sensitivity and Survey Potential of a Commercial Metal Detector Mounted on a Robotic Platform for Safely Detecting Plastic Landmines</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:title>
   <dc:subject>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>CEIA MIL-D1; humanitarian demining; detection performance; metal detector characterization; buried metallic targets; electromagnetic induction; mine action technology</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:subject>
   <dc:type>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>Text</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:type>
   <dc:description>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">The widespread presence of landmines and unexploded ordnance in the Ukraine conflict zone has created an urgent need for reliable sensing technologies capable of supporting autonomous and semi-autonomous humanitarian demining. As part of a multi-sensor approach</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">The widespread presence of landmines and unexploded ordnance in the Ukraine conflict zone has created an urgent need for reliable sensing technologies capable of supporting autonomous and semi-autonomous humanitarian demining. As part of a multi-sensor approach</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">metal detectors (MDs) remain one of the primary sensors for locating buried explosive hazards. In this work we present an experimental characterization of the CEIA MIL-D1 MD using inert mine simulants of four common types of plastic landmines encountered in the Ukraine conflict zone (the PFM-1 ``butterfly'' mine</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">metal detectors (MDs) remain one of the primary sensors for locating buried explosive hazards. In this work we present an experimental characterization of the CEIA MIL-D1 MD using inert mine simulants of four common types of plastic landmines encountered in the Ukraine conflict zone (the PFM-1 ``butterfly'' mine</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">PTM-1G ``stick'' anti-tank (AT) mine</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">PTM-1G ``stick'' anti-tank (AT) mine</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">the TM-62P3 AT mine</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">the TM-62P3 AT mine</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and the PMN-4 mine). We investigated the MD efficacy in a configuration in which the coil was mounted at a fixed height and offset from a robotic platform being operated remotely</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and the PMN-4 mine). We investigated the MD efficacy in a configuration in which the coil was mounted at a fixed height and offset from a robotic platform being operated remotely</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">a configuration which is safer for the operator than the standard use of the MD with lateral sweeping by hand. We conducted controlled laboratory experiments to measure the MD's two-dimensional detection footprint as a function of sensor (coil) height and lateral offset from the coil center. We produced quantitative detection regions for each target</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">a configuration which is safer for the operator than the standard use of the MD with lateral sweeping by hand. We conducted controlled laboratory experiments to measure the MD's two-dimensional detection footprint as a function of sensor (coil) height and lateral offset from the coil center. We produced quantitative detection regions for each target</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">which informs how well we can detect these targets in an idealized situation. Our measurements were complemented by field experiments in a realistic test lane containing these same mine simulants along with representative metallic clutter objects. At our nominal MD operating height of 11 cm</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">which informs how well we can detect these targets in an idealized situation. Our measurements were complemented by field experiments in a realistic test lane containing these same mine simulants along with representative metallic clutter objects. At our nominal MD operating height of 11 cm</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">the detector reliably detected 3 of the 4 investigated mine simulants in the test lane. The PFM-1 mine was only sporadically detected</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">the detector reliably detected 3 of the 4 investigated mine simulants in the test lane. The PFM-1 mine was only sporadically detected</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">but this surface mine can be detected visually via cameras and automated object recognition. Our results indicate that maintaining a target distance within approximately 10 cm of the centerline of the MD coil provides high-confidence detections</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">but this surface mine can be detected visually via cameras and automated object recognition. Our results indicate that maintaining a target distance within approximately 10 cm of the centerline of the MD coil provides high-confidence detections</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">suggesting a conservative survey lane width of 20 cm</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">suggesting a conservative survey lane width of 20 cm</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">while a 30 cm lane width may be acceptable under similar field conditions. This study establishes a quantitative performance baseline for the CEIA MIL-D1 MD undergoing linear motion in a fixed mounted configuration</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">while a 30 cm lane width may be acceptable under similar field conditions. This study establishes a quantitative performance baseline for the CEIA MIL-D1 MD undergoing linear motion in a fixed mounted configuration</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and constrains lane-planning parameters and ground survey efficiency for our multisensor humanitarian demining system.</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and constrains lane-planning parameters and ground survey efficiency for our multisensor humanitarian demining system.</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:description>
